havior, physical activity, and the metabolic syndrome among U.S. adults.</article-title><source>Obes Res</source><volume>13</volume><fpage>608</fpage><lpage>614</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15833947</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006117-Li1"><label>9</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ford</surname><given-names>ES</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Definition of the Metabolic Syndrome: What's New and What Predicts Risk?</article-title><source>Metab Syndr Relat Disord</source><volume>4</volume><fpage>237</fpage><lpage>251</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18370743</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006117-Britton1"><label>10</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Britton</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Is there an answer? How did the idea of breeding rats for intrinsic aerobic running capacity come about?</article-title><source>IUBMB Life</source><volume>55</volume><fpage>429</fpage><lpage>430</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14584594</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006117-Koch1"><label>11</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Koch</surname><given-names>LG</given-names></name><name><surname>Britton</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Artificial selection for intrinsic aerobic endurance running capacity in rats.</article-title><source>Physiol Genomics</source><volume>5</volume><fpage>45</fpage><lpage>52</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11161005</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="pone.0006117-Wisloff1"><label>12</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><s